  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1246 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nUnseasonably Warm and Dry Through Saturday...\nAn amplified upper-level ridge is centered over the Great Lakes\ntoday and will translate east to the Atlantic Coast through \nSaturday. At the surface, a 1028mb high is centered over the \nUpper Ohio Valley this afternoon and also translates slowly east\nto New England by Saturday. To our west, an elongated surface \nlow is in place from the Dakotas into the Central Great PLains. \nIn between these features, expect persistent southwesterly flow \nacross central Illinois driving well above normal temperatures \nwith daytime highs reaching around 70 degrees under mostly sunny\nskies and overnight lows remaining mild in the 40s to lower \n50s.\n\nTurning Colder Sunday Into Next Week...\nMeanwhile Saturday, a pair of vigorous shortwaves -- one \ndigging out of the Canadian Prairies and another across the \nDesert Southwest -- are forecast to phase as they move east \nacross the mid and upper Mississippi Valley on Sunday. In \nresponse the elongated surface low will deepen slightly and push\nslowly east across the region, with a cold front pushing across\ncentral Illinois Sunday morning through the afternoon and \nbringing our next chance for widespread light rain. While weak \ninstability may accompany the cold front (GFS suggests pockets \nof 50 J/kg MUCAPE), poor mid-level lapse rates shown in \nforecast soundings should limit updraft depth below the ice \ngrowth zone, keeping thunderstorm potential minimal. Overall QPF\nis expected to be light, offering minimal relief for the \nongoing regional drought. NBM 24-hour mean QPF targets \nwidespread amounts around 0.25-0.50 inches, trending up slightly\nfrom earlier runs. Isolated totals around an inch remain \npossible (90th percentile) where localized updrafts can leverage\nthe limited instability. The best chance for these higher \ntotals is near or west of the I-55 corridor.\n\nBehind the cold front, a 1035 mb surface high will build over \nthe northern Great Plains Sunday night and expand into the Upper\nMidwest Monday and Tuesday. A tight northerly pressure gradient\non the leading edge of the high will funnel colder air \nsouthward into central Illinois, driving Sunday night lows down \nnear or just below freezing and limiting Monday highs to the 40s\nnorth of I-70. Under the influence of the high, quiet and dry \nconditions will prevail as temperatures dip slightly further \nthrough midweek.\n\nLonger-range guidance continues to signal a storm system \nimpacting the region Wednesday into Thursday of next week. \nHowever, substantial spread remains among guidance, with latest \ndeterministic runs of the GFS and ECMWF exhibiting notable \nevolution and timing discrepancies regarding energy phasing \nbetween northern stream and southern stream shortwaves. Given \nthe considerable model divergence including some precip-type \nuncertainty this far out, confidence remains low. Maintained NBM\nbaseline slight chance to low chance PoPs (15-30%), focused \nprimarily across east-central Illinois. Forecast details will be\nrefined as model guidance converges over the coming days.",
